stitute in centro scopia  with a group of people who wanted to  learn turkish within on an intensive  course right so that at least i wouldn't  feel that i was going very slowly  and i actually really enjoyed it because  i got insights into  because they were all from local  uh people macedonia yeah i was getting  input from them from how they saw the  turkish language affect their own  language  they were sharing and of course you  speak macedonians yeah of course yeah so  the course was only in masada well the  course was supposed to be just in target  language in turkish right but if there  were ever any explanations needed  they would be given in macedonia right  and that wasn't a problem so i was sort  of doing the sort of outside class we  tell a chat in macedonia and or whatever  um but i found that really interesting  and how many hours a day or how many  times a week and it's 16 hours a week  oh that's a lot yeah yeah and was that  the sum total of your effort or did you  then go home and do other things no i  mean you always use it so i i tend to  practice as much as possible so i'd find  and seek out turkish speakers in town  and practice it with them and i'd listen  to bits of things on on tv turkish news  or whatever i could find right and you  know i believe as well in a lot of  inputs so right you need a lot of um  sort of time to sort of exposure to the  language right not just  one source and is your input primarily  than audio input because you're talking  to people and they're kind of the  language is coming back at you or did  you also read in turkish so in the bit i  mean in the beginning  the the reading you can do is limited of  course yes but um yeah i do i do  different things so  now for example with turkish i may i may  read subtitles i might have a program  running english right when they have the  subtitles in turkish or i may watch  something in turkish have subtitles in  in macedonia or  in in serbian or in another language  that i speak  um well right  and that way i find that it just sort of  keeps things interesting  but the classes really were fundamental  to bringing me on because i had very  definite goals  i was taken out of my comfort zone which  i think sometimes as a language learner  especially if you're autodidactic  you you can you can look so much in on  yourself  that you will only learn things that  you're really interested in right but  when you have a conversation with a  language partner  of course you you have to there's a  sharing right to go on they might  mention things that you've never heard  of  things that you're not interested in  right so the language class i found  because you had different personalities  right helped to really bring those  things out right now of course you were  in macedonia  learning turkish which is a language  that is spoken there  now what if you're learning a language  or have you been in a situation where  you are learning a language where there  are no no speakers of the language  locally uh there may not even be courses  offered locally uh what do you do then  then normally what i do is get a book i  like course books i like to read through  and get an idea of the language so  in that way i guess i may be a more  typical polyglot because i think a lot  of polyglots do tend to do that kind of  thing they'll just get a book and work  through it as though they were in class  but what i will normally do is  supplement that with um a teacher online  so from italki  or find  a speaker  and i will um  maybe not always use the book exactly as  it's prescribed i would read a dialogue  and then i would make that dialogue  relevant to my own life  so when i was learning norwegian for a  trip to norway  i took the teach yourself a norwegian  book  i  read it over the course of about a month  and then each lesson with my teacher i  meet once a week and i tell her the unit  i'd studied to talk about what happened  this is online this is it online yeah  yeah talk about the what happened in the  book what happens with the the  characters in the book and then say but  in my life  this is what happened to me  so i'd make it relevant to myself right  and then i went to norway and practiced  with people and  chatted away so yeah it's  but still there's an element of teaching  there because you're sort of you're  making up your own teacher environment  yeah no and uh you know i certainly have  shared that experience for example with  check where i study check on my own but  with the goal of going to prague to  interact with people and activate it and  also of course i use online tutors  and try to  get them to talk about for example say  lessons that i'm studying  you know a link and whatever and so it  is similar to that extent although i'm i  tend  i do use teach yourself as a place to  get started in the language exactly but  then i tried to get into a range of you  know content uh you know that i can work  but  um  to what extent do you think the  polyglots how much of their learning  methodology is common and to what extent  do they have sort of their own  individual  approaches i think many people have very  different approaches  but they're the one thing that they they  really have in common is  the  sort of dyna so i guess motivation it  sounds a bit right that's right i guess  yeah yeah yeah yeah yeah yeah it's there  yeah but it's um  it is important because they have the  motivation from the general um and very  concentrated interest  right it's very focused interest in  right in learning it and  they question language i think  so you know if you i guess you would  maybe do this as well but if you walk  down the street yes you've been learning  czech you walk down the street in prague  you see a word you don't know  and what do you do do you even look at  it and think about it no no just ignore  it  so even then maybe maybe that it's still  different i don't know  let's put it this way i enjoy the fact  that i'